Z||000000||ponya||12-18-2000||07:25 AM||||My son and I have reached just about the end of our patience and our sanity. If you have read my other post, this is about mushy brakes. Yesterday, we did everything that was advised by the forum to try to get the brakes to stop properly. After several hours of bleeding and testing, I still cannot drive it becuse I dont trust it to stop in an emergency situation. We even took off the diverter valve and cleaned it out and re-installed it. It checked out in the static test. After all that and several quarts of brake fluid, we are back where we started. Is it time to seek professional help? Or does someone have anything else to suggest I try. Sorry for the long post, but we are both out of ideas. Thanks for you patience,  Bob||||reg||9
Z||000001||fiero56||12-18-2000||08:52 AM||||I've got the same problem, haven't found a fix yet. To stop I have to push the pedal to the floor, let it up, them pump it several times, and then I can stop(not on a dime mind you, but it will stop <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||12-18-2000||09:02 AM||||Have either of you checked your power booster and lines?||||reg||1
Z||000003||lawrence||12-18-2000||02:07 PM||||You might check to see that your rotors are not warped.  If they are, when you roll down the street, the rotors tend to push the piston back into the cylinder bore more than they should.  Then, much of your pedal stroke is just to get the pistons back out to the rotors.<P>Also, make sure your sliders are good and free.  When you turn, the rear hubs flex quite a bit.  Again, if the sliders are stiff, when the hubs flex, the piston is pushed into the bore rather than the entire caliper floating on the slider pins.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||12-18-2000||03:26 PM||||What Fiero56 is describing, sounds like possibly the master cylinder going south. I've had a couple vehicles in the past that the first pump of the brake pedal felt scary but then it would pump up and work ok. Both times it was a bad M/C and both times I was not losing brake fluid (it was leaking past the internal seals, but not past the outer ones).||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-18-2000||06:54 PM||||This started happening when I replaced a rear caliper. Then decided to replace both front calipers and master cylinder. Now it is terrible. I have plans to replace both rear calipers, but $$$, epescially for '88 calipers.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-18-2000||07:55 PM||||Have you tried the thing I put in the other thread yet? This is a common problem with these tilted master cylinders.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bhl46||12-18-2000||10:29 PM||||Make sure the bleeders are at the at the top of the caliper. There is a left and a right side,but they will bolt on either side.If they are on the wrong side the bleeder will be on the bottom.You can bleed them all day and not get the air out.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||PlasticWrapd||12-19-2000||06:48 AM||||Couple of quick things...  Did you bench bleed the master cylinder before installing it?  Like Ogre said, master cylinders mounted on an angle can trap air.  Also check the parking brake adjusters on the rear calipers.  Make sure the screws turn freely.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||12-19-2000||01:40 PM||||Ogre, can't find you post in another thread? Could someone leade me to it?<P>I did bench bleed the master, but might try it again sometime.||||reg||1
